    thinking: "**Discovering `generate_image` Capabilities**\n\nI\'ve just realized something exciting! The `generate_image` tool isn\'t as limited as I initially thought. I see now that it can accept an array of `ImagePaths`, according to the tool description. It looks like you can pass in existing images to it. This opens up new possibilities.\n\n\n**Analyzing Image Data**\n\nI\'m now focusing on the implications of image paths and how to retrieve user-provided images. Initially, I was concerned about accessing those images, but I realize the user\'s uploaded image path may already exist, or that we can generate one. Crucially, the user prompt clarifies that they want to *use* the provided image directly, rather than any modifications. This is key, I\'ll need to figure out what image paths we have. The user provided an image of a shirtless man and another of a man in a professional suit. It appears they want the shirtless image, but replaced with their face/the suit image! The user seems unhappy, that the bot is still modifying the image from its initial request to \"change to this image don\'t configure anything to the image I shared.\"\n\n\n**Locating User Uploads**\n\nI\'m now zeroing in on locating the user\'s uploaded image. It seems the platform may save images within `/src/assets/images`, but I\'m also considering alternative storage locations, to be sure I\'ve exhausted options. I\'m exploring how to identify the specific file path of the image the user wants to use, given that the user does not want the image to be modified. I\'m going to list the contents of the directory again to refresh myself. I\'ll need to use `list_dir`.\n\n\n**Confirming Image Persistence**\n\nI\'ve made a breakthrough. I found the original image path in `Hero.tsx`, and I\'ve verified that we mistakenly overwrote the intended image by generating a new one. It turns out that the user\'s uploaded image was intended as the base, not an image to be changed. I realized that the user was unhappy. I will now revert back to the original image.\n\n\n"
